                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Frame the problem & audit the assumptions`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The board has issued an ultimatum: you spread MosaicQA across <strong>four markets</strong> and validated none. Separate the <em>problem worth solving</em> from the <em>solution you happen to have built</em>. Write the problem in customer language, then build an assumption ledger — the ~20 beliefs the venture depends on — and rank each by <strong>impact-if-wrong × evidence</strong>. The technical founder wants to spend <strong>C$120,000 — four months of runway</strong> — hardening the MVP before talking to more customers. With 13 of 20 assumptions untested and a US rival who just raised US$4M, is that the move? Flag the leap-of-faith assumptions first, and decide how many markets you can actually afford to carry.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — AI-augmented ideation: choose the path`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Engineering isn't your bottleneck — the model was built in 11 weeks. Your scarcity is <em>validated knowledge of who will pay</em>. You have 25 minutes and three AI-augmentation modes: <strong>Divergent</strong> (expand the option space, then human-filter), <strong>Convergent</strong> (humans shortlist, AI red-teams), or <strong>Synthetic-customer</strong> (AI simulates buyer objections). Each has a failure trap. Maxwell's discipline: AI <strong>supercharges structured creativity</strong> but does not replace the human who filters for genuine novelty and field-truth. Used naively it raises idea <em>volume</em> while lowering idea <em>novelty</em>. Set a novelty bar — and name what a human added that a rival running the same prompt wouldn't get for free.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Cheap validation sprint`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `You have a fixed <strong>C$25,000 and 4 simulated weeks</strong> to convert leap-of-faith assumptions into evidence. Choose from the menu — 15 problem interviews (C$3k), a smoke-test landing page (C$6k), a paid pilot / LOI push (C$12k), a wizard-of-oz concierge service (C$10k), a field test on a real line (C$9k) — to retire the <strong>most risk per dollar</strong>. Spread C$6,250 across four markets and you get weak signal in all four and conviction in none; <em>concentrate</em> and you get hard evidence on the one market you'd bet the company on. The interviews surface a signal card: the real budget-holder is the <strong>quality manager</strong>, not the plant owner. Update the ledger honestly — persevere or pivot.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit to one route & defend the runway`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Day 90. Choose <strong>one</strong> commercialization route and kill the other two out loud: <strong>direct paid pilots</strong> with extruders (slow, high-margin, defensible), a <strong>channel/OEM embed</strong> (fast reach, margin surrendered), or a <strong>self-serve defect API</strong> (scalable, unvalidated demand, longest time-to-revenue). Build a runway-aware plan: a defensible <strong>time-to-MVP</strong>, and a <em>funding-unlock milestone</em> the next C$90k actually buys in proof. An MVP isn't a milestone investors fund — a paying customer is. Refuse to kill any route and you recreate the exact diffusion that triggered the ultimatum. Pitch the board.`,

                    r1_problem_label:       `Write the problem statement. Whose language is it in?`,
                    r1_leap_label:          `First move: build the polished MVP, or audit the assumption ledger?`,
                    r1_focus_label:         `The board forced a focus decision. How many candidate markets do you carry forward?`,
                    r1_focus_readout:       `Candidate markets still in play:`,
                    r1_focus_of:            `/ 4`,
