                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_customer:      `<strong>Good — that's the problem in the buyer's words.</strong> A quality lead measured on defect escape rates has a number you can attach to; "we have a machine-vision model" is a solution looking for a problem. Now make sure you test that belief before you build for it.`,
                    coach_r1_feature:       `<strong>That's a feature, not a problem.</strong> "We can ship faster because the model took 11 weeks" describes your supply, not anyone's pain. Engineering speed is exactly what isn't scarce here — validated demand is. Reframe in the customer's words.`,
                    coach_r1_product:      `<strong>That's product language, and it's the trap.</strong> "MosaicQA detects micro-defects" is what you built, not the problem worth solving. The board wants the customer's pain, not your model's spec sheet — who is measured on this, and how badly?`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp founder instinct.</strong> You framed the problem in customer language AND chose to audit assumptions before burning a dollar on build. That is exactly the structured discipline the board is forcing — now concentrate the markets so the audit isn't spread four ways.`,
                    coach_r1_parallel:      `<strong>"Do both" is build-first wearing a disguise.</strong> A smaller build still burns runway against demand you haven't tested, and hedging across four markets is the diffusion that triggered the ultimatum. Audit first — the cheapest experiment beats the prettiest prototype.`,
                    coach_r1_build:         `<strong>You just committed C$120,000 — four months of runway — to hardening MosaicQA.</strong> You now have a beautiful product and 13 of 20 assumptions still untested. A US competitor just raised US$4M. When I ask "who has agreed to pay for this?", what's your answer, and how many months of cash have you got left to find one?`,
                    coach_r1_fourmarkets:   `<strong>Four markets is exactly what got you here.</strong> "Optionality is now a liability." Carrying all four dilutes your effort and your runway 4-to-1 — pick the customer you will starve to serve, not the four you'll half-serve.`,
                    coach_r2_divergent:     `<strong>Divergent with a human filter is the right use of AI.</strong> Let the model blow open the option space, then you cut for novelty and feasibility — that's structure supercharged, not judgment outsourced. Just watch the volume trap: more ideas isn't more signal.`,
                    coach_r2_convergent:    `<strong>Convergent red-teaming is a strong, disciplined choice.</strong> Humans generate the shortlist, the AI stress-tests it — you keep ownership of what's on the list while getting a faster adversary. Make sure you don't narrow so early you cut the non-obvious wedge.`,
                    coach_r2_synthetic:     `<strong>Synthetic-customer testing buys you cheap objections before you spend real validation budget.</strong> Used with judgment it pre-empts the interview surprises — just remember a simulated buyer can't sign a cheque, so treat it as a hypothesis, not evidence.`,
                    coach_r2_oracle:        `<strong>Your AI gave you 40 ideas in 90 seconds and you shipped the top 5 it ranked.</strong> So did every other founder with the same prompt. Your novelty score is stuck at 2 out of 5. What did a <em>human</em> on this team add that a competitor running the same model wouldn't get for free? Right now, nothing.`,
                    coach_r2_nohuman:       `<strong>You picked a structured mode but no human owns the novelty filter.</strong> A high target on the slider is just a number if no one on the team can say what makes an idea non-obvious. AI supercharges structure — it doesn't supply the judgment. Name the human edge.`,
                    coach_r2_synthtrap:     `<strong>Synthetic customers breed false confidence when no human checks them.</strong> The AI will happily simulate a buyer who loves your product. Field-truth comes from a real quality manager with a budget — keep the judgment human or you'll mistake a confident simulation for demand.`,
                    coach_r2_volume:        `<strong>Prettier slides on AI output isn't a human edge.</strong> Divergent mode inflated your option count; packaging it more cleanly doesn't make any of it non-obvious. The human's job is to cut the generic 90%, not gift-wrap it.`,
                    coach_r3_concentrate:   `<strong>Concentration is the discipline under scarcity.</strong> The full C$25k on the one market you'd bet the company on buys hard evidence, not a fuzzy read on four maybes. Now make sure your mix includes a willingness-to-pay signal, not just interviews.`,
                    coach_r3_spray:         `<strong>You put C$6,250 into each of four markets.</strong> That buys weak signal in all four and conviction in none. The board wanted <em>one</em> defensible plan. With C$25,000, would you rather have a fuzzy read on four maybes or hard evidence on the one market you'd stake the company on?`,
                    coach_r3_pivot:         `<strong>You read the signal card and re-aimed at the real buyer.</strong> Plant owners say "nice to have"; the quality manager has budget and a number — defect escape rates. Rewriting the value proposition around their metric is the difference between a user and a buyer. The ledger is honest again.`,
                    coach_r3_note:          `<strong>"Revisit the buyer after the first deal" is how you sell to the wrong person for six months.</strong> The signal card is telling you who signs the cheque <em>now</em>. Who you sell to isn't a detail to defer — it's the assumption your whole pipeline rests on.`,
                    coach_r3_wrongbuyer:    `<strong>Your interviews show plant owners say "nice to have" and quality managers say "I have budget."</strong> You've kept selling to owners because that was your original assumption. Who actually signs the cheque — and does your value proposition speak to their number, defect escape rates?`,
                    coach_r3_nointerviews:  `<strong>You want to chase the quality-manager signal but you funded no interviews.</strong> Interviews are how that buyer surfaces at all. You can't pivot to evidence you never gathered — put real budget into talking to people.`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>A workable mix — but tilt it toward signal-per-dollar.</strong> Interviews surface the buyer cheaply; a paid pilot or LOI is the strongest willingness-to-pay signal. The more your budget retires the riskiest assumption, the more the next round writes itself.`,
                    coach_r4_direct:        `<strong>Direct paid pilots are the defensible beachhead.</strong> Slow and high-margin, but each deal is real evidence and a reference for the next. It's the route the validated data supports — now tie a time-to-MVP and a paying milestone to it so the board sees the proof, not just the plan.`,
                    coach_r4_oem:           `<strong>An OEM embed buys reach fast but surrenders margin and control.</strong> It can work if the validation pointed there — but you've handed the customer relationship to the vendor, and your defensibility now lives in someone else's roadmap. Defend why the speed is worth the control you gave up.`,
                    coach_r4_api:           `<strong>The self-serve API is the scalable dream and the longest road to revenue.</strong> It's on-brand for an AI-built team, but if the validation didn't show real demand it's the co-founder's pet route the data doesn't support. Scalable demand you can't yet evidence is still an assumption, not a plan.`,
                    coach_r4_all:           `<strong>Day 90 and you're still "exploring" all three routes.</strong> That's exactly what triggered the ultimatum six months ago. Optionality costs runway. Which two routes will you say "not now" to — out loud, to me — so the one you keep gets all the cash?`,
                    coach_r4_tooaggressive: `<strong>An MVP in three weeks isn't an MVP — it's a demo.</strong> You'll show the board something that breaks on a real line and call it proof. A defensible time-to-MVP is long enough to be true and short enough to leave selling time. Three weeks is neither.`,
                    coach_r4_toolong:       `<strong>Sixteen-plus weeks of hardening and you've sold nothing before the next raise.</strong> That's most of your remaining runway spent building against demand you should be proving in parallel. Shorten it, or the milestone that unlocks the C$90k slips past your cash-out date.`,
                    coach_r4_milestone:     `<strong>That's the milestone investors actually fund.</strong> "3 paying pilots at C$5k MRR by a named date" is evidenced willingness-to-pay tied to the raise — direct pilots that prove the beachhead and unlock the next C$90k. The runway now buys proof, not just product.`,
                    coach_r4_vanity:        `<strong>10,000 signups isn't a milestone — it's a vanity metric.</strong> Top-of-funnel numbers impress no one who's read a cohort table. The board funds evidenced willingness-to-pay, not impressions. What does the next C$90k <em>buy</em> in proof a paying customer would recognise?`,
                    coach_r4_mvpmilestone:  `<strong>"An MVP shipped" isn't a milestone investors fund — a paying customer is.</strong> You've promised a product, not proof. What does the next C$90k buy your board, and on what date does a real buyer say yes?`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
