                    insight_won:            `Beachhead won: you framed the problem in the customer's words, kept human ownership of the AI-generated novelty, concentrated the validation budget on one market and committed to one route with a paying milestone. The board has the evidence-backed plan it demanded — the C$90k tranche unlocks and the next raise has a real story.`,
                    insight_optionality:    `Optionality trap: you reached Day 90 still keeping routes open — the exact diffusion the ultimatum was meant to end. The board sees four half-served maybes instead of one defensible beachhead, and the tranche stays frozen. Commitment, not optionality, was the deliverable.`,
                    insight_runway:         `Out of runway: too much cash burned on building before validating left you without the months to prove a paying customer. An MVP with no buyer and a near-empty bank account is the build-first trap closing — the scarcest resource was always the clock.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully retire the riskiest assumptions. The plan still rests on beliefs you never tested cheaply — enough for the board to ask "who has agreed to pay?" and not like the answer.`,
                    insight_buildfirst:     `You committed to building before validating. Hardening the MVP burned runway against untested demand — the classic build-first error. The cheapest experiment would have told you more than the prettiest prototype, for a fraction of the cash.`,
                    insight_lownovelty:     `Your idea-novelty stayed low. Treating AI output as the answer produced high-volume, low-novelty options indistinguishable from any rival running the same prompt. AI supercharges structure — but only a human supplies the non-obvious wedge.`,
                    insight_spray:          `You spread the validation budget too thin. C$6,250 across four markets bought weak signal everywhere and conviction nowhere — concentration, not diversification, is the discipline under scarcity.`,
                    insight_wrongbuyer:     `You kept selling to the wrong buyer. The signal card revealed the quality manager holds budget, not the plant owner — and you stayed with your original assumption. "Who pays" was itself an untested belief, and it cost you.`,
                    insight_evidenced:      `Strong evidence base: you retired the riskiest assumptions cheaply and your venture risk is low. The next funding conversation rests on proof, not optimism — exactly the position a pre-seed founder wants before a US-funded rival forces the timeline.`,

                    hint_runway:            `Your runway is dangerously low. Every week of building before validating burns ~C$7k — stop hardening the product and put the remaining cash into proving a paying customer.`,
                    hint_optionality:       `You're still carrying multiple routes. Optionality costs runway — name the two routes you'll kill out loud so the one you keep gets all the cash and all the focus.`,
                    hint_novelty:           `Your idea-novelty is low — the AI gave you what it gave everyone. Choose a structured mode and name the human field-truth judgment that makes one option non-obvious.`,
                    hint_wrongbuyer:        `Evidence says the quality manager holds the budget, not the plant owner. Re-aim the value proposition at the person measured on defect escape rates before you build the pitch around the wrong buyer.`,
                    hint_validate:          `Too few assumptions tested with little budget left. Concentrate on one beachhead and buy the strongest willingness-to-pay signal — a paid pilot or LOI beats four fuzzy reads.`,
                    hint_risk:              `Venture risk is high. Frame in customer language, validate before building, and tie a paying milestone to the route — each retires unpriced risk the board is counting.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — assumptions evidenced, risk low, runway intact. Lock in one route with a paying milestone and defend killing the others to win the tranche.`,

